hi this is Mario with Mario's math  tutoring coming to with another math  video to help you boost your score in  your math class improve your  understanding and hopefully make  learning math a lot less stressful so in  this video what we're going to do is  we're going to talk about some more  difference quotient examples okay so to  give you some more practice and just  give you some more understanding about  how to do the difference quotient so if  you want you can pause the video and go  ahead and try to do the difference  quotient here on your own and then we'll  go through these together so just  another way to get some extra practice  so what I'm going to do here is I'm  going to find the difference quotient of  f of X equals 1 over X minus 5 so just a  quick refresher this is the difference  quotient here and students get a little  bit confused sometimes about you know  what goes in for what right you know  what substitution are we making and let  me see if I can just show you how this  works so whatever is in the parentheses  okay that's the input that's what you're  putting into your function in place of X  so we're going to do is going to go to  our function okay right here and  whatever is in the parentheses that's  our input that's what goes in for X okay  on the right side so we're going to be  replacing X with X plus h so this is  going to be 1 over X plus h minus 5  minus okay that's this minus here f of X  and I just think of that as f of X is  just this whole function the original  function so 1 over X minus 5 divided by  H and it's the limit as H is approaching  0 okay now you don't want to make this  mistake you don't want to put 0 in too  soon if you do you're going to get 0  over 0 which is an indeterminate form  which means that you know we can't  really you know really make any  conclusions about this at this point so  we really want is we want to simplify  this down further okay before we  substitute 0 in for H so what we're  going to do here is we're going to  multiply by the common denominator so  what we're doing is we're gonna multiply  by X plus h minus 5 and X minus 5 but  whatever you do to the numerator we want  to do to the denominator right because  we want to keep this equivalent so we  just want them this is like multiplying  by the number 1 so what I'm going to do  here is I'm going to distribute this  quantity to this fraction and to this  fraction okay so you're with me so far  so when we do that we  distribute that to this fraction the X  plus h minus five and this X plus h  minus five they're going to cancel one  another out you're just going to have X  minus five times one which is X minus  five minus right when we distribute this  to this fraction the X minus 5s are  going to cancel out because this is in  the numerator this is in the denominator  right so we're just going to have 1  times X plus h minus 5 now you want to  be careful because when you're  subtracting you're really subtracting  this entire thing so some students  forget to put the parentheses you want  to treat this as a group okay and you're  subtracting this entire group okay and  then the last step here is we're just  going to multiply this whole thing times  the denominator but don't foil the  denominator just leave it in factored  form and you'll see why in a minute  because we're going to get some  cancellation that occurs okay so now  what we can do is we can distribute this  negative okay so that's going to be X  minus 5 minus X minus H plus 5 so we're  just distributing like so and that's all  over H times X plus h minus 5 times X  minus 5 and you can see the X's are  cancelling the 5s are cancelling we're  just left with negative H over H and now  what you can do is you can divide these  both by H you're just going to be left  with negative 1 over X plus h minus 5  times X minus 5 it's the limit as H  approaches 0 we can put 0 now in for H  and we're just left with negative 1 over  X minus 5 times X minus 5 which is X  minus 5 squared so this is our  difference quotient and again remember  what the difference quotient is this is  a formula it's a general formula for  finding the slope ok the rate of change  at any point along this graph so if we  wanted to find well what's the slope of  the tangent line when X is 4 right it's  going to be 4 minus 5 which is negative  1 negative 1 squared is 1 negative 1  divided by 1 is negative 1 so it's going  to have a negative 1 slope but if we  were to do it at let's say when X is 10  you know you're going to get a different  slope because right you know the graph  is not going  at a continuous rate so let's look at  another example let's look at this one  over here f of X equals x cubed plus two  okay so we're going to go through that  same exact process so what we're going  to do is we're going to substitute X  plus h in for X okay now if there was  another X here right here we would also  put X plus h in for that X as well so  wherever whatever is inside this  parentheses is going to go in for X on  the right side of the equation okay  that's how functions work this is the  input and the f of X is the y-value  that's the output but in this case we  just have one X here so what I'm going  to do is I'm going to place X with X  plus h the quantity cubed plus 2 okay so  that's that's this part here minus f of  X which is the original function that's  this whole thing right here so minus X  cubed plus 2 all divided by H that's the  limit as H is approaching 0 so now we're  going to do is we're going to expand  this out X plus h cubed ok if you  remember Pascal's triangle I'll go over  here remember Pascal's triangle 1 1 1 2  1 1 3 3 1 ok these are the coefficients  we're going to use this to expand so  this is actually going to be X cubed  plus 3x squared h plus 3x h squared plus  h cubed plus 2 minus X cubed minus 2 I'm  just distributing the negative all  divided by H now if you want to see this  you can do this another way too you  could write this as X plus h 3 times ok  oil these two together get an answer  then distribute the X and then  distribute the H simplify and you're  going to get this but using Pascal's  triangles gonna be a lot faster okay so  now we're just going to simplify the X  cubes cancel because ones positive one  is negative the 2s and the negative 2's  are going to cancel and you notice how  each term each group has an H in it so  what we're going to do is we have after  that each out okay so we're going to  factor the H out this is going to be 3x  squared plus 3x H plus h squared all  divided by H  and you can see the HS are canceling a  numerator and denominator all right so  now remember it's the limit as H is  approaching zero if I put zero in for H  this whole term is going to be zero and  this is going to be zero and we're left  with three x squared so this is our  difference quotient simplified and  remember what the difference quotient is  it's actually a general formula okay for  finding the slope anywhere along this  graph so if I wanted to find the slope  of the tangent line at x equals one  right the slope would be 1 squared is 1  times 3 is 3 so we would have a you know  an incline at that point of 3 and  instantaneous rate of change so I hope  this helped you I hope you are  understanding how to work with the  difference quotient better I'll have  some links and the cards for some of my  other videos regarding the same topic  and I look forward to seeing you in some  of the future videos I'll talk to you  soon

